# Title: How the crow drives
# Kind: scene
# Synopsis: Kyle has sixty minutes to thread a fresh-transplant kid through every checkpoint and gray zone between the south sprawl and Northwestern Memorial. As the crow flies. Or in his case, drives.

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped title:"The wrong intersection"
The ambulance T-bones a stalled hauler at the Hegewisch crossroads, two lanes south of a Crucible Genomics auxiliary clinic, and the back doors swing open like the universe wants Kyle to see what's inside. Which is rude, because thirty seconds ago Kyle was having a perfectly normal disagreement with an Arcturus contractor about whether the contractor was going to shoot Kyle today. The contractor decided yes. The contractor then decided no, because the contractor decided to take a shotgun blast for his trouble — somebody else's blast, not Kyle's; Kyle is a man of standards. Kyle rolls under the resulting noise, comes up clean, and finds himself looking at the back of an ambulance and a small face inside it. The small face waves.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
"Are you the new one," the small face asks, and Kyle understands the question is not the one she's asking. She's eight, maybe nine, her chest is taped under a flexible biopolymer brace, and she is more composed than the second-tier corponation security officer currently bleeding out a meter to Kyle's left. Kyle steps up into the bay. Driver is dead. Medic in the passenger seat is also not coming back. The patient tag on the gurney reads IMANI OKONKWO-LINDQVIST. RECIPIENT. POST-OP, FIRST 6 HOURS. NORTHWESTERN MEMORIAL. Kyle reads it twice. The math does itself. He says, "Hi, Imani. I'm Kyle." She says, "I know. My uncle's friend has your hat."

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:tense pace:clipped
He thumbs the tag's emergency contact. One ring. "Vance," a voice says, already running. Kyle says, "Your driver's dead. The kid is on the floor of a south-side ditch with a bioprinted heart wired to a battery. I have a motorcycle." Silence. Then Vance says, "What's the brace reading." Kyle looks. "Sinus, ninety-two, paced." Vance says, "You have sixty minutes before the vascular scaffold throws a clot. Maybe seventy. The wires are not decoration." Kyle says, "Northwestern." Vance says, "Streeterville. Trauma bay nine. I'll have a slot." Kyle says, "I'm in Hegewisch." Vance does the same math he just did. "Straight line. North. Don't stop for checkpoints." Kyle says, "Have you met me." Vance hangs up.

%% gap 900

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped facet:MASK
Twenty-two kilometers. Sixty minutes. The Pulse hyperloop is faster and Pulse will not stop for a man with a child on the back of a bike. Ferrogate's surface rail is owned by a corponation with a four-page list of reasons not to open a door. Every artery worth using is a sovereign zone with a tollgate and an attitude. The route his nav suggests is forty-two minutes through Tollgate-owned bridges. Kyle does not have time to pay tolls. Kyle does not, in fact, *enjoy* paying tolls. So. As the crow flies. Or — and he likes this thought, he likes it a lot — as the crow *drives*.

%% gap 1200

%% beat tone:tender pace:clipped
He lifts Imani. She weighs less than his jacket. He sets her sidesaddle on the pillion, threads the medical harness through his belt-loop so the battery rides over his kidney instead of swinging, ties her wrists loosely through the back of his coat so she will not fall off if she sleeps. Her cheek finds the back of his shoulder like it has been there before. "Hold on," he says, which is a stupid thing to say to a kid whose hands are too small to grip his ribs, but it is what you say. She says, "How fast." He says, "Fast." She says, "Good." The bike starts. Eight cylinders' worth of audacity in a city that does not deserve them.

%% gap 1500 scene-end chapter:"Section I: South of everything"

%% beat tone:excited pace:flowing
The first kilometer is gray zone — Hegewisch sprawl, no sovereign in residence. Kyle takes Avenue O at one-thirty, threading two rows of collapsed light poles. He does not signal. There is no one to signal to except a flock of crows on the wires, who do not vote on the matter. Imani's breathing is a small warm patch against his back, even, slow, deliberately controlled. He says, "You're not scared." She says, "I'm being efficient with my fear." Kyle laughs out loud. The crows lift off.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:wry pace:staccato chapter:"Section II: Crucible Genomics — checkpoint"
The checkpoint is staffed by two guards in soft-helmet armor and a third behind a blast-shield. They have the bored faces of people who expect a manifest. Kyle does not have a manifest. He has a child and a battery and a smile. He brakes hard, throws his hands off the bars in the universal *not-a-threat* posture, and shouts, "POST-OP TRANSIT, LACUNA COOLER, NORTHWESTERN," before the guards can finish their reach. Left guard looks at the brace. Right guard looks at the cooler logo. The guard behind the glass — Crucible internal, white coat under the armor — looks at Imani's face. She raises one hand and waves. The guard's expression goes through three weather systems in one second. "Let him through," he says. "Phenotype clearance." The barrier sinks. Kyle says, "Have a great evening," and he means it.

%% gap 700

%% beat tone:wry pace:flowing
Two and a half kilometers of Crucible private artery, manicured, palm-lit, river on the right. Kyle pegs the throttle. The bike makes the unsubtle music of a thing built before noise ordinances. Workers in white smocks look up from the greenhouses and decide not to look long. The corporation has classified Imani as outgoing inventory, which means Kyle is allowed to drive through Eden as long as he keeps moving. He keeps moving. Imani says, "Why are the trees in rows." Kyle says, "Because the company likes to know where things are." She says, "That seems anxious." Kyle has nothing to add. The kid is correct.

%% gap 1000

%% beat tone:excited pace:clipped
"Hold tight through the gate," he says over his shoulder. "Loud part coming." She says, "I like loud." Kyle laughs. "Then this is your whole night." The gate is the north exit, ten meters wide, two armored arms folding open and closed on a four-second cycle. He times it. The bike clears the second arm with twenty centimeters to spare. They drop off the corporate pavement onto the broken asphalt of a gray zone and the suspension finds out about Chicago all at once. The kid laughs too. The sound is small and clean and goes through Kyle like a tuning fork.

%% gap 1500 scene-end chapter:"Section III: Pullman ruins — gray zone"

%% beat tone:wry pace:flowing
Pullman is a graveyard with opinions and a garden. The old factory town fell off the corporate map twenty years ago and got reclaimed by squatters, scavengers, three different micro-gangs who all think they own the same intersection, and a community of bicyclists who have built an honest-to-god vegetable plot in the train turntable pit. Kyle threads between a burning barrel and a tarp city. Two kids look up. One of them recognizes the bike, recognizes *him*, and shouts something Kyle cannot hear over the engine but absolutely is the word *legend*. Kyle raises one hand in greeting like he is in a parade. In a sense he is.

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:tender pace:clipped
Imani's hand finds the side of his coat and clutches. Not afraid — the grip is steady, intentional — she is *noticing*. She has not been outside Crucible's pavement before. Kyle understands this without being told. He says, "First time off the block?" She says, "First time on the ground." He says, "Well. Buckle up. The ground is the best part." She says, "Buckle up to what." He says, "Existentially." She thinks about it. She says, "Okay."

%% gap 700

%% beat tone:tense pace:staccato chapter:"Section IV: Ashgrave Corridor — the toxic kingdom"
The Ashgrave Synthesis Corridor announces itself with a wall of haze you can taste before you see it. The perimeter lights glow the wrong color — too yellow, too low — the way a refinery glows when it is having a feeling. Kyle has a respirator clipped to his jacket. Imani does not. He reaches back without slowing, finds her face with his palm, presses his bandana against her mouth. "Breathe shallow. Through this. Not your nose." She nods against his hand. He bites his own respirator's mouthpiece, snaps the seal, and rides into a sky the color of old bruises like he was invited.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:tense pace:staccato
Two refinery towers exhale orange fire on either side of him, close enough to read rivet patterns. The air pressure shifts as a vent stack twenty meters above blows downward. The bike yaws. He corrects. Imani's grip becomes a small steel claw at his belt. He thinks, *I have been here before in a dream and I did not enjoy it then either.* Out loud he says, "We're fine." She says, through the bandana, "I know. I can tell from your shoulders." Kyle has met adults who could not read his shoulders. He is starting to take this kid personally.

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:violent pace:staccato
Halfway through, an Ashgrave patrol crawler — six wheels, plasma cutter mounted for stripping abandoned vehicles, not technically for people, but the distinction is academic — drops out of a side road and pulls into the lane behind him. Loudspeaker: "Unauthorized vehicle. State manifest." Kyle does not state. Kyle accelerates. The crawler is not built to chase a bike but it is *built to deny*. The cutter swivels. Kyle weaves left, right, left. The cutter fires a warning slash across the pavement six meters ahead, vaporizing a strip of asphalt into orange vapor that smells like burning teeth. Kyle rides through the vapor. The cutter cycles to recharge. Three seconds. He needs two. He likes those odds.

%% gap 500

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
He clears the Corridor's north gate at one-eighty, comes off the entrance ramp without slowing, spits out the respirator, and pulls Imani's bandana down. "You with me?" She coughs once, twice, then says, "I'm with you. Your shoulders said." He says, "My shoulders are working overtime." She says, "Give them a raise." Kyle laughs so hard he almost loses the line.

%% gap 1500 scene-end chapter:"Section V: Englewood gray zone — the ambush"

%% beat tone:tense pace:flowing
Englewood at night looks like a city that has been told a joke nobody is laughing at *yet*. Half the streetlights work. The other half are someone's collateral. Kyle takes 63rd west, intending to cut north on Halsted where the asphalt is the least cratered. He is wrong about Halsted being safe and he is wrong by about thirty seconds.

%% gap 500

%% beat tone:violent pace:staccato
Two motorcycles peel out from a gas station that has not pumped gas in eighteen years. Then a third from behind a bus shell. **Vultures-adjacent — the dropouts, the ones who couldn't make crew chief Stash's cut.** They want a bike, a coat, anything they can sell to the actual Vultures by morning. The first one pulls alongside, swings a chain at Kyle's head. Kyle ducks. The chain catches his shoulder pauldron and rings like a bell. He says, conversationally, "That was your first mistake."

%% gap 400

%% beat tone:wry pace:staccato
He cannot fight on the bike. Imani is on the back. Every dodge yanks her medical battery against his hip and that is the only vote that matters. He scans for an exit. There is no exit. There is only forward, and forward has another two of them coming up the cross street. Kyle says, over his shoulder, "Two minutes." Imani says, "Take three." He laughs and brakes hard.

%% gap 400

%% beat tone:violent pace:staccato
The bike shrieks. The pursuers overshoot, brake, circle back. Kyle pulls Imani off the pillion as if she weighs nothing and sets her against the brick wall of a boarded-up barbershop. "Stay. I owe you ninety seconds." She says, "I'm counting." He turns. Silence comes off his back in the motion of a man unbuttoning a coat. The samurai stance happens because the body remembers. He pulls the strop across the blade once on the way up — left forearm, the way it has always been done — and the blade comes off the strop already singing.

%% gap 400

%% beat tone:violent pace:staccato
First one swings the chain again. Kyle takes the chain out of the air and the wrist with it. Second one tries for a pistol. Silence is faster than a draw. He does not kill him. He cuts the holster off, clean, leather and plastic in two pieces. The pistol falls. Kyle steps on it. Third one — the smart one — drops his bike and runs. Fourth and fifth are still coming up the cross street. Kyle looks at them. They look at him. They look at the two on the ground who are not getting up but are not exactly *dead* either, which somehow reads worse. They look at Kyle's coat. They turn around and ride the other way. Kyle says, to their tail-lights, "Tell Stash hi."

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
He sheathes. He kneels at the bike. The chain wrapped the front spokes when the first one hit him and the wheel is now an interpretive sculpture. The frame is fine. The wheel is over. He has thirty-eight minutes and no front wheel. He says, "Hm." Imani says, "Are we okay." He says, "We are about to be opportunistic."

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
The first pursuer's bike is on its side eight meters away, idling, owner currently rethinking life choices and his wrist. Kyle picks the bike up. He picks Imani up. He says, "Plot twist." She says, "Is it ours now?" He says, "It's ours until somebody bigger asks for it back." He fits her on the pillion, reroutes the medical harness through this bike's frame, kicks the kickstand up. The pursuer makes a noise from the ground. Kyle says, "I'll mail it back. With a card."

%% gap 1500 scene-end chapter:"Section VI: Cinderfall thermal grid — the heat"

%% beat tone:wry pace:flowing
The stolen bike is louder than his was and runs harder on the corners. He likes it. He is not going to keep it but he likes it. He takes it north up Halsted, threads between a fallen billboard and a stalled bus, and the air rising off the pavement starts to bend. Cinderfall Energy runs eleven subterranean thermal installations under this stretch of the corridor; the surface above them is a grid of steam-vent grates and convection waves and asphalt that has been re-laid three times this year because it keeps warping. Imani says, "Why is the air doing that." Kyle says, "Because the corporation under us is cooking." She says, "Oh." It is the *oh* of a child who has just learned that the world has a basement.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:mysterious pace:staccato chapter:"Section VII: Anomaly — physics declines to participate"
He should go two blocks west. Two blocks west is Bridgeport, which is normal. He goes straight because straight is what the call is for, and at 35th and Halsted the world stops being a place. The bike's headlight hits something and the something does not hit back. Kyle's stomach drops a meter without him. The wheels are still on pavement but the pavement is half a second behind where his eyes say it is. Imani makes a sound he has not heard a child make before — not fear, not pain. *Recognition*. As if she has been in a room like this in her head.

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:mysterious pace:languorous
This is one of the thin spots the network maps with a triangle and the word LATENT and a footnote about insurance. Three blocks square. Inside it, gravity is a suggestion and so is the sequence of things. Kyle does not slow because slowing in a thin spot is how you become a story other people tell. He keeps the throttle even. He fixes on a point ahead — the far light, a streetlamp on the north edge — and does not look at anything else, especially not the things on the periphery that may or may not be people and may or may not be in the same minute as him. He says, "Don't look left." Imani says, "I already didn't."

%% gap 700

%% beat tone:afraid pace:staccato
Something *moves* in the periphery anyway. Low, four-limbed, wrong shoulder geometry. A mutant — one of the things the network does not officially admit live in the thin spots. It launches across the street at the bike. Kyle does not see it; he hears Imani's intake of breath, which is the only warning he needs. He swings the bike right. It is the wrong move because in a thin spot *right* is sometimes *left* and *left* is sometimes *down*. The mutant misses by a meter that should have been a centimeter. Kyle does not understand the geometry of his own escape and does not bother. The far streetlamp is closer. The throttle stays even. The world remembers its rules a block later. He is through. He says, "*Rude*." Imani says, "Agreed."

%% gap 1500 scene-end

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped chapter:"Section VIII: The Lobby War"
Twenty-two minutes. He hits the south edge of the Loop at speeds municipal law has strong opinions about, except municipal law has been on wildcat strike for nine days and currently has *no* opinions, which Kyle considers a generous gift. The most direct route through the Mirrorwell Arcology is, in fact, *through* the Mirrorwell Arcology. The building has a service tunnel under its full footprint, six lanes wide, badge-locked at both ends. The Loop municipal floors — one, two, three — own the lower entry. The corponation owns four and up. Between them is a famously dysfunctional arrangement locals call the Lobby War. Kyle is about to vote.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
The municipal gate is open because the city is on strike and there is nobody at the kiosk. The Mirrorwell gate at the north end is closed and has guards. He enters the tunnel. The space is cathedral-tall and full of advertising on every surface, scrolling sideways like a low river of light. Imani says, "It's beautiful." Kyle says, "Don't tell the advertisers, it'll go to their heads." He rides the median. He hits the north end at eighty.

%% gap 500

%% beat tone:wry pace:staccato
The Mirrorwell guards have a thermal lock on him three seconds before he arrives. They cycle the gate open because the policy on civilian incursion is *let through, charge later, send invoice*. They are not Arcturus. They are not paid to fight. They are paid to take pictures and forward billing. Kyle blows past at speed, blinded once by a flash, and is on the north side of the Loop in a gray zone that smells like the river. He says, "Send the invoice to the ghost." Imani says, "What ghost." He says, "We'll get to it."

%% gap 700

%% beat tone:tense pace:clipped chapter:"Section IX: River North — the underpass"
River North gray zone is a thirty-second wedge between the Mirrorwell footprint and the Waxwing Spire District. It is mostly flooded. The Chicago River has not been managed in eighteen years, and Lower Wacker is a canal now. He takes the upper level. The upper level is a bridge that Tollgate Systems owns and charges credits to cross. Kyle does not have a Tollgate badge. Kyle has thirty seconds and an opinion about Tollgate.

%% gap 400

%% beat tone:wry pace:staccato
The Tollgate barrier is a swinging steel boom. He hits it at forty. The boom snaps clean off its hinge and goes spinning into the river like an unimpressed sword. The bike scrapes the railing on the right, throws sparks up into the dark, recovers. A camera tracks them across the span and sends an invoice to a credit account that has not existed since 2207 — Kyle made the account specifically for situations like this, then deleted it. The invoice will get an automated rejection. The rejection will be filed. The filing will be archived. The archive will be deleted in a routine purge. The whole exchange will take six weeks and cost Tollgate seventeen credits in processing. He laughs. Imani says, "Why are you laughing." He says, "Because the most expensive thing I did to them tonight was the bookkeeping." She says, "Good."

%% gap 1200 scene-end chapter:"Section X: Streeterville — Waxwing & the hospital"

%% beat tone:tense pace:flowing
Streeterville at this hour is a forest of glass towers wearing crowns of advertising. The Waxwing Spire is the tallest. Northwestern Memorial is at its feet — a brutalist medical megastructure that does not match anything around it because it has been there since the twenty-tens, which is somehow still inside the corponation's tolerance for old buildings, possibly because the corponation makes a lot of money knowing exactly when each patient inside it dies. Kyle does not care. Kyle sees Trauma Bay Nine's red light from six blocks out. The bay door is already up. Two figures in scrubs are standing in the lane with a gurney. One of them must be Vance.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:tender pace:clipped
He brakes. The bike does not enjoy this. He skids the last ten meters and arrives sideways at the bay door. Vance — tall, brown skin, hair tied back, scrubs over a body that has run a lot today — does not waste a syllable. "Get her on the gurney." Kyle is already lifting. The medical harness comes off in two practiced motions. Imani's hand stays in his coat for a half-second after the rest of her is on the gurney. He has to pry it loose. She says, "Thank you for the loud." He says, "Anytime, kid. Tell the heart I said hi."

%% gap 800

%% beat tone:wry pace:clipped
Vance reads the brace, the pacing wires, the cooler. Her face does the math. "Forty-three minutes," she says. "I had you at fifty-five." Kyle says, "I cheated." She says, "You were supposed to. Stay. We may need a blood draw." He says, "I don't stay." She looks at him for one long beat. She nods, because she has met his kind. The gurney goes inside. The bay door drops. The red light goes white.

%% gap 1500 scene-end chapter:"Section XI: Crow's flight"

%% beat tone:wry pace:languorous
Kyle leans against the stolen bike. The bike — not his bike, his bike is a pretzel in Englewood — ticks as it cools. The Waxwing Spire glows above him, scrolling an ad for cognitive enhancement that he is absolutely not in the market for tonight. A crow lands on the railing across the street. It looks at him. He looks at it. The crow makes the small dry noise crows make when they are thinking. Then it lifts and takes the straight line north over the lake.

%% gap 600

%% beat tone:happy pace:clipped
Kyle salutes it. "Showoff," he says. The bike starts. He turns it south, the long way, because south is no longer urgent. He has somewhere to be. He always does. Halfway home he passes the spot in Englewood where his old bike is still a pretzel and the pursuer is still rethinking his life choices. Kyle slows. The pursuer flinches. Kyle says, "I'm gonna keep this one. Tell yourself a good story about why." He throttles up. The crow is already three lakes ahead of him. He doesn't mind. The crow doesn't have to brake for tolls.
